WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.760
Youth recruitment by terrorist groups is a
global challenge.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.190
This is particularly true in countries with
a youth bulge—where there is a disproportionate

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.919
number of individuals under the age of 30.

00:00:15.919 --> 00:00:20.800
Many such countries have already seen significant
terrorist activity, including Afghanistan,

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.619
Nigeria, and Yemen.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:26.380
The United States needs a better response.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.439
U.S. efforts thus far have focused on countering
terrorists’ propaganda, primarily via the

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:32.439
internet.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:34.930
This only addresses one part of the problem.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:40.290
3% of surveyed violent extremists in Africa
reported joining a group through internet

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.310
contacts.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.790
Roughly 60% joined through a friend or family
member.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.800
The United States must shift its counterterrorism
resources accordingly.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.800
Families should be seen for what they are:
a social network that can both impede

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:57.010
or facilitate the spread of violent extremism.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:01.650
Targeted and tailored outreach programs should
engage with high-risk families to counter

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:06.820
terrorists’ exploitation of youth, because
children’s lives are on the line.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:12.140
Should countering youth radicalization be a part of US counter-terrorism strategy?

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.900
Let us know in our poll.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.700
Also let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.880
sure to like and subscribe for more research
and videos from AEI.

